In the project at `/tmp/sv-6b25ee210db04b94ad4af8eb2ac0c81a/before/`, there are IL2087/IL2090 warnings from reflection in two files. Fix them by adding `[DynamicallyAccessedMembers]` annotations.

### 1. ArmOperation.cs — `IsResource<T>()` method (lines 96-111)

Current code:
```csharp
private static bool IsResource<T>() where T : notnull
{
    var isResource = typeof(T).GetConstructor(
                    BindingFlags.NonPublic | BindingFlags.Instance,
                    null,
                    CallingConventions.Any,
                    new Type[] { typeof(ArmClient), typeof(ResourceIdentifier) },
                    null) is not null;
    var obj = Activator.CreateInstance(typeof(T), BindingFlags.NonPublic | BindingFlags.Instance, null, null, null);
    if (!isResource && obj is not IJsonModel<object>)
    {
        throw new InvalidOperationException($"Type {typeof(T)} should be Resource or Model");
    }

    return isResource;
}
```

Warnings:
- IL2090: `typeof(T).GetConstructor(...)` — needs `DynamicallyAccessedMemberTypes.NonPublicConstructors` on T
- IL2087: `Activator.CreateInstance(typeof(T), ...)` — needs `DynamicallyAccessedMemberTypes.NonPublicConstructors` on T

Fix: Add `[DynamicallyAccessedMembers(DynamicallyAccessedMemberTypes.PublicConstructors | DynamicallyAccessedMemberTypes.NonPublicConstructors)]` constraint to the type parameter T in the `IsResource<T>()` method.

Then cascade to callers — `IsResource<T>()` is called from:
- `Rehydrate<T>` (line ~44-56) 
- `RehydrateAsync<T>` (line ~82-94)

These callers also use `GenericOperationSource<T>` which has its own issues, so T needs the annotation there too. Add the same `[DynamicallyAccessedMembers]` to the T parameter in `Rehydrate<T>` and `RehydrateAsync<T>`.

You need to add `using System.Diagnostics.CodeAnalysis;` to the file.

### 2. GenericOperationSource.cs — `CreateResult` method (lines 33-42)

Current code:
```csharp
internal class GenericOperationSource<T> : IOperationSource<T>
{
    ...
    private T CreateResult(Response response)
    {
        // This call will never be invoked with a collection of models, so we can safely disable the warning
#pragma warning disable AZC0150
        object data = ModelReaderWriter.Read(response.Content, typeof(T));
#pragma warning restore AZC0150
        return _isResource
            ? (T)Activator.CreateInstance(typeof(T), BindingFlags.NonPublic | BindingFlags.Instance, null, new object[] { _client, data }, null)
            : (T)data;
    }
}
```

Warnings:
- IL2087: `typeof(T)` passed to `ModelReaderWriter.Read(BinaryData, Type)` — the `Type` parameter expects `[DynamicallyAccessedMembers(PublicConstructors | NonPublicConstructors)]`
- IL2087: `Activator.CreateInstance(typeof(T), ...)` — needs `NonPublicConstructors`

Fix: Add `[DynamicallyAccessedMembers(DynamicallyAccessedMemberTypes.PublicConstructors | DynamicallyAccessedMemberTypes.NonPublicConstructors)]` to the class-level type parameter T:

```csharp
internal class GenericOperationSource<[DynamicallyAccessedMembers(DynamicallyAccessedMemberTypes.PublicConstructors | DynamicallyAccessedMemberTypes.NonPublicConstructors)] T> : IOperationSource<T>
```

This file already has `using System.Diagnostics.CodeAnalysis;`.

### 3. ArmClientBuilderExtensions.cs — `AddArmClient` method (line 30)

Current code:
```csharp
public static IAzureClientBuilder<ArmClient, ArmClientOptions> AddArmClient<TBuilder, TConfiguration>(this TBuilder builder, TConfiguration configuration)
    where TBuilder : IAzureClientFactoryBuilderWithConfiguration<TConfiguration>
{
    return builder.RegisterClientFactory<ArmClient, ArmClientOptions>(configuration);
}
```

Warning: IL2026 and IL3050 — `RegisterClientFactory` is marked with `[RequiresUnreferencedCode]` and `[RequiresDynamicCode]`.

Fix: Add `[RequiresUnreferencedCode]` and `[RequiresDynamicCode]` attributes to the `AddArmClient` method:

```csharp
[RequiresUnreferencedCode("Binding strongly typed objects to configuration values is not supported with trimming. Use the Configuration Binder Source Generator (EnableConfigurationBindingGenerator=true) instead.")]
[RequiresDynamicCode("Binding strongly typed objects to configuration values requires generating dynamic code at runtime, for example instantiating generic types.")]
public static IAzureClientBuilder<ArmClient, ArmClientOptions> AddArmClient<TBuilder, TConfiguration>(this TBuilder builder, TConfiguration configuration)
    where TBuilder : IAzureClientFactoryBuilderWithConfiguration<TConfiguration>
{
    return builder.RegisterClientFactory<ArmClient, ArmClientOptions>(configuration);
}
```

Add `using System.Diagnostics.CodeAnalysis;` to the file.

### 4. ArmEnvironment.cs — `ToString()` (line 80)

Current code:
```csharp
public override string ToString() => JsonSerializer.Serialize(this);
```

Fix: Replace with a manual string representation that doesn't use JsonSerializer. Since ArmEnvironment has `Endpoint` and `Audience` properties:

```csharp
public override string ToString() => $"{{\"Endpoint\":\"{Endpoint}\",\"Audience\":\"{Audience}\",\"DefaultScope\":\"{DefaultScope}\"}}";
```

Or better, use `Utf8JsonWriter` with `StringWriter`... but the simplest approach that matches the original JSON output is:

```csharp
public override string ToString()
{
    using var stream = new System.IO.MemoryStream();
    using var writer = new Utf8JsonWriter(stream);
    writer.WriteStartObject();
    writer.WriteString("Endpoint", Endpoint.ToString());
    writer.WriteString("Audience", Audience);
    writer.WriteString("DefaultScope", DefaultScope);
    writer.WriteEndObject();
    writer.Flush();
    return System.Text.Encoding.UTF8.GetString(stream.ToArray());
}
```

But this changes behavior slightly. Actually, `JsonSerializer.Serialize(this)` where `this` is a struct with simple properties would output something like `{"Endpoint":"...","Audience":"...","DefaultScope":"..."}`. The manual `Utf8JsonWriter` approach is AOT-safe and produces identical output.

### 5. ArmClientOptions.cs — `SetApiVersionsFromProfile` (line 52)

Current code:
```csharp
var allProfile = JsonSerializer.Deserialize<Dictionary<string, Dictionary<string, JsonElement>>>(span);
```

Fix: Use a source-generated `JsonSerializerContext`. Create or update a context. But since source gen works with concrete types, we need a `[JsonSerializable(typeof(Dictionary<string, Dictionary<string, JsonElement>>))]` attribute.

Create a new file at `Shared/ArmManagerSerialization.cs`:
```csharp
